1.0 0.0.0.255 echo-reply\n    - interface <int>; ip access-group PERMIT-ICMP in\n  - On ASA/FTD, validate with packet-tracer:\n    - packet-tracer input <in-if> icmp <src-ip> 8 0 <dst-ip>\n- If the remote worker uses VPN/SD-WAN, verify tunnel reachability and policies:\n  - show crypto ikev2 sa; show crypto ipsec sa\n  - show vpn-sessiondb anyconnect / show sdwan control connections\n  - Ensure split-tunnel and crypto ACLs include 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/24.\n- Re-test and validate:\n  - ping 10.0.1.2 and 10.1.0.2 from both the remote worker and the server\n  - Collect outputs (traceroute, show ip route, show access-lists) if failure persists for escalation.', 'Tracking Next Steps': '- After fixes, run continuous pings from each source: remote-worker -> 10.0.1.2 and server -> 10.1.0.2 (e.g., ping -i 1 for 15–30 minutes); target is 0% packet loss.\n- If any loss persists, run traceroute/mtr from both sources and capture the hop where it fails.\n- Monitor network devices: interface status/error counters, link flaps, ARP/MAC table stability on access switches, and firewall/router logs for ICMP or related drops; track NMS up/down status.\n- Check reachability of other hosts in 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/24 to determine host-specific vs network-path issues.\n- Escalate if still failing:\n  - Only 10.0.1.2 or 10.1.0.2 down while others in subnet are reachable: escalate to server/VM team to verify power, OS, NIC/port, and IP conflicts.\n  - Multiple hosts unreachable or traceroute fails beyond local gateway/WAN: escalate to network team or ISP/SD‑WAN with ping/traceroute outputs, timestamps, and interface stats.\n  - Issue isolated to remote-worker path: escalate to VPN/endpoint team.\n- Closure criteria: 30–60 minutes with 0% packet loss from both sources, no interface errors or flaps, and green status in monitoring.', 'Alarm Details': 'ping from remote-worker: PING 10.0.1.2 (10.0.1.2): 56 data bytes; 4 packets transmitted, 0 packets received, 100% packet loss | ping from 