    "Recommended Actions": "- Verify the destination hosts are online and allowed to reply to ICMP:\n  - On 10.0.1.2 and 10.1.0.2: confirm NIC is up, correct IP/mask/gateway; ping their default gateway.\n  - Temporarily allow ICMP on OS firewalls (Windows: netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name=\"ICMP Allow\" protocol=icmpv4:8,any dir=in action=allow; Linux: sudo iptables -I INPUT -p icmp --icmp-type echo-request -j ACCEPT).\n- From each source, test local connectivity:\n  - ping <default-gateway>\n  - traceroute 10.0.1.2 / traceroute 10.1.0.2 to locate the first failing hop.\n- On the Layer-3 gateway(s) for 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/24, verify interfaces/VLANs are up:\n  - show ip interface brief\n  - show vlan brief\n  - show run interface vlan <VLAN-ID> (confirm SVI IPs and no shutdown)\n  - show interfaces status (confirm access/trunk ports carry the correct VLANs)\n- Check ARP and MAC reachability for the destinations:\n  - show arp | include 10.0.1.2\n  - show arp | include 10.1.0.2\n  - clear arp <ip> and retry ping if stale/incomplete.\n- Validate routing on transit routers/firewalls:\n  - show ip route 10.0.1.2\n  - show ip route 10.1.0.2\n  - If routes are missing, add the correct routes or advertise via the IGP:\n    - ip route 10.0.1.0 255.255.255.0 <next-hop>\n    - ip route 10.1.0.0 255.255.255.0 <next-hop>\n    - or configure OSPF: router ospf 1; network 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.255 area 0; network 10.1.0.0 0.0.0.255 area 0\n- Inspect ACLs/firewall policies that might block ICMP between the sources and 10.0.1.2/10.1.0.2:\n  - show access-lists\n  - show run interface <int> (check ip access-group in/out)\n  - Add explicit permits if needed (example):\n    - ip access-list extended PERMIT-ICMP\n      permit icmp 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.255 10.1.0.0 0.0.0.255 echo\n      permit icmp 10.1.0.0 0.0.0.255 10.0.1.0 0.0.0.255 echo-reply\n    - interface <int>; ip access-group PERMIT-ICMP in\n  - On ASA/FTD, validate with packet-tracer:\n    - packet-tracer input <in-if> icmp <src-ip> 8 0 <dst-ip>\n- If the remote worker uses VPN/SD-WAN, verify tunnel reachability and policies:\n  - show crypto ikev2 sa; show crypto ipsec sa\n  - show vpn-sessiondb anyconnect / show sdwan control connections\n  - Ensure split-tunnel and crypto ACLs include 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/24.\n- Re-test and validate:\n  - ping 10.0.1.2 and 10.1.0.2 from both the remote worker and the server\n  - Collect outputs (traceroute, show ip route, show access-lists) if failure persists for escalation.",
    "Tracking Next Steps": "- After fixes, run continuous pings from each source: remote-worker -> 10.0.1.2 and server -> 10.1.0.2 (e.g., ping -i 1 for 15\u201330 minutes); target is 0% packet loss.\n- If any loss persists, run traceroute/mtr from both sources and capture the hop where it fails.\n- Monitor network devices: interface status/error counters, link flaps, ARP/MAC table stability on access switches, and firewall/router logs for ICMP or related drops; track NMS up/down status.\n- Check reachability of other hosts in 10.0.1.0/24 and 10.1.0.0/24 to determine host-specific vs network-path issues.\n- Escalate if still failing:\n  - Only 10.0.1.2 or 10.1.0.2 down while others in subnet are reachable: escalate to server/VM team to verify power, OS, NIC/port, and IP conflicts.\n  - Multiple hosts unreachable or traceroute fails beyond local gateway/WAN: escalate to network team or ISP/SD\u2011WAN with ping/traceroute outputs, timestamps, and interface stats.\n  - Issue isolated to remote-worker path: escalate to VPN/endpoint team.\n- Closure criteria: 30\u201360 minutes with 0% packet loss from both sources, no interface errors or flaps, and green status in monitoring.",
    "Alarm Details": "ping from remote-worker: PING 10.0.1.2 (10.0.1.2): 56 data bytes; 4 packets transmitted, 0 packets received, 100% packet loss | ping from server: PING 10.1.0.2 (10.1.0.2): 56 data bytes; 4 packets transmitted, 0 packets received, 100% packet loss",
    "Insights": "100% packet loss from both hosts to different IPs suggests a broader network connectivity issue or ICMP/firewall blocking.",
    "Priority": "Urgent",
    "Status": "Active",
    "Alarm Source": "Ping Result",
    "Alarm ID": "Ping Result 1"
}
